TI  - Cortical thickness, surface area and folding in patients with psychogenic nonepileptic seizures
T2  - EPILEPSY RESEARCH
M3  - Article
AB  - Objective: To determine cortical thickness (CTh), cortical surface area (CSA), curvature sulcal depth (SD) in patients with psychogenic nonepileptic seizures (PNES).Methods: Freesurfer software was used to identify differences between active and group in Cth, CSA, curvature, and SD. Neuropsychological tests intending to document frontal lobe deficit were applied.Results: We included 37 patients with PNES (age 37.3 +/- 13.8; female/male 31/6; age of onset 26.1 +/- 10.6; age of disease duration 11.1 +/- 11.1), and 37 healthy controls (age +/- 12.7; female/male 26/11). No difference in CSA and curvature was detected between Patients with PNES had increased CTh in the left insula, left and right medial-orbitofrontal, left lateral-orbitofrontal, and decreased CTh in the left and right precentral, right enthorinal, and right lateral-occipital region than healthy controls. SD was increased at the level of left and right insula, right rostral anterior cingulate, right posterior cingulate, and left cuneus, and reduced at the level of the right and left medial-orbitofrontal sulci in patients with compared to healthy controls.Conclusion: Individuals with PNES display a distinct profile of changes in CTh, in association increase in SD in both insula as compared to controls. Our results may contribute to the standing of the neurobiological background of PNES. Further research, to include replication the findings and directed to understand the role of insula is needed. (C) 2015 Elsevier B.V. All rights reserved.
