TI  - Gait disorders of aging: Falls and therapeutic strategies
T2  - Gait disorders of aging: Falls and therapeutic strategies
M3  - Book
AB  - This compendium presents up-to-date information regarding the prevalence, morbidity, mortality, and physiology of gait disorders along with subsequent injuries in the elderly. Treatment methods are also discussed. It comprises twenty-four individually authored chapters intended for neurologists, internists, geriatricians, physiatrists, nurses, and physical therapists. The text identifies those individuals at risk, and it discusses prevention strategies while addressing recent advances in the study of locomotion. The effects of disease and normal changes in gait with aging are also examined. Other topics discussed include balance assessment, motor control evaluation, classification of gait disorders, and foot and ankle abnormalities affecting gait. Clinical features, epidemiology, and treatment for such disorders as cervical spondylotic myelopathy, parkinsonism, cerebrovascular and cerebellar disorders, and proprioception disorders are addressed. Psychogenic gait disorders, anxiety, and the fear of falling are evaluated. Methods to improve balance and increase strength in order to reduce the risk of falling in the elderly are examined as well as environmental factors such as lighting, floor surface improvements, footwear, and ambulation devices. Incorporated into the well-referenced chapters of this text are photographs, radiographs, MRI images, drawings, charts, tables, and graphs. Two appendices for the axial mobility exercise program and performance-oriented environmental mobility screen are provided at the end of the text. An index is also provided.
