TI  - Initial Preclinical Evaluation of 18F-Fluorodeoxysorbitol PET as a Novel Functional Renal Imaging Agent
T2  - JOURNAL OF NUCLEAR MEDICINE
M3  - Article
AB  - Accurate assessment of kidney function plays an essential role for optimal clinical decision making in a variety of diseases. The major intrinsic advantages of PET are superior spatial and temporal resolutions for quantitative tomographic renal imaging. 2-deoxy-2-F-18-fluorodeoxysorbitol (F-18-FDS) is an analog of sorbitol that is reported to be freely filtered at the renal glomerulus without reabsorption at the tubule. Furthermore, it can be synthesized via simple reduction of widely available F-18-FDG. We tested the feasibility of F-18-FDS renal PET imaging in rats. Methods: The systemic and renal distribution of F-18-FDS were determined by dynamic 35-min PET imaging (15 frames x 8 s, 26 frames x 30 s, 20 frames x 60 s) with a dedicated small-animal PET system and postmortem tissue counting in healthy rats. Distribution of coinjected Tc-99m-diethylenetriaminepentaacetic acid (DTPA) was also estimated as a reference. Plasma binding and in vivo stability of F-18-FDS were determined. Results: In vivo PET imaging visualized rapid excretion of the administrated F-18-FDS from both kidneys, with minimal tracer accumulation in other organs. Initial cortical tracer uptake followed by visualization of the collecting system could be observed with high contrast. Split-function renography curves were successfully obtained in healthy rats (the time of maximal concentration [T-max] right [R] = 2.8 +/- 1.2 min, T-max left [L] = 2.9 +/- 1.5 min, the time of half maximal concentration [T-1/2max] R = 8.8 +/- 3.7 min, T-1/2max L = 11.1 +/- 4.9 min). Postmortem tissue counting of F-18-FDS confirmed the high kidney extraction (kidney activities at 10, 30, and 60 min after tracer injection [percentage injected dose per gram]: 1.8 +/- 0.7, 1.2 +/- 0.1, and 0.5 +/- 0.2, respectively) in a degree comparable to Tc-99m-DTPA (2.5 +/- 1.0, 1.5 +/- 0.2, and 0.8 +/- 0.3, respectively). Plasma protein binding of F-18-FDS was low (<0.1%), and metabolic transformation was not detected in serum and urine. Conclusion: In rat experiments, F-18-FDS demonstrated high kidney extraction and excretion, low plasma protein binding, and high metabolic stability as preferable properties for renal imaging. These preliminary results warrant further confirmatory studies in large animal models and clinical studies as a novel functional renal imaging agent, given the advantages of PET technology and broad tracer availability.
