TI  - New L-asparaginase mutant obtained by replacing            threonine at specific position in specific amino acid            sequence with glutamine, used for preparing L-aspartic            acid
AB  - 
                   NOVELTY - An L-aspartase mutant, is new, where threonine                at position 221 in a 278 amino acid sequence (SEQ                ID NO: 1) fully defined in the specification is                replaced with glutamine.
                    USE - The L-asparaginase mutant is used for                preparing L-aspartic acid (claimed).
                    ADVANTAGE - The L-aspartase mutant has specific enzyme                activity of 170.6 U.mg-1, which is close to the                wild type; has no change in the optimal temperature                and optimal pH; and has improved thermal stability                (after treatment at 50&#8451; for 30 minutes,                the residual enzyme activity of mutant is 92.16%,                while the wild-type residual enzyme activity is                only 52.9%). The transformation rate of the mutant                single-enzyme engineering strain is significantly                improved compared to the starting strain, where the                2 hours transformation rate increased from 67.1% of                the starting strain to 93.2%, the transformation                completion time is advanced from 5 hours to 3                hours, and the productivity increased from                47.92-79.86 g.L-1.hour-1.
                    DETAILED DESCRIPTION - INDEPENDENT CLAIMS are included for:a nucleotide sequence encoding the L-aspartase                mutant;a recombinant expression vector comprising the                nucleotide sequence, where the recombinant                expression vector comprises pET-24a(+);a gene engineering bacterium comprising the                recombinant expression vector, where the                genetically engineered bacterium is a recombinant                strain obtained by connecting the nucleotide                sequence to a vector, and then introducing the                recombinant expression vector into a host bacterium                for inducing expression, and the host bacterium                comprises , or yeast; andEscherichia coliBacillus                subtilisa preparation method of L-aspartic acid,                comprising taking fumaric acid as substrate, adding                the gene engineering bacteria, and performing                enzyme conversion reaction to obtain L-aspartic                acid, where, when performing the enzyme conversion                reaction, the genetically engineered bacteria are                purified.
