TI  - Making ornithine aspartate involves carrying out            polymerase chain reaction amplification with            recombinant plasmid, transforming vector into E. coli            to form strain, catalyzing substrates, enzymatic            conversion, decompressing and concentrating
AB  - 
                   NOVELTY - Forming ornithine aspartate involves                subjecting aspartase gene from Bacillus subtilis to                polymerase chain reaction (PCR) amplification and                making recombinant expression plasmid                pET-20b(+)-asp; carrying out PCR amplification with                the plasmid; making recombinant plasmid                pET-24a(+)-arg-asp or pET-20b(+)-asp; transforming                recombinant expression vector into Escherichia coli                BL21 to form co-expressed genetic engineering                strain BL21 (DE3)-pET24a(+)-arg-asp; with strain,                catalyzing substrates, then by enzymatic                conversion, clearing, decoloring, decompressing and                concentrating.
                    USE - For preparing ornithine aspartate through                co-expression of arginase and aspartase                (claimed).
                    ADVANTAGE - The method is simple; and has mild reaction                condition, short period, enzymatic system, little                impurity, convenient separating and extracting                product, safe operation, and good industrial                application prospect.
                    DETAILED DESCRIPTION - Preparing ornithine aspartate through                co-expression of arginase and aspartase involves                subjecting the aspartase gene from Bacillus                subtilis to polymerase chain reaction (PCR)                amplification and constructing recombinant                expression plasmid pET-20b(+)-asp by the genetic                engineering method; then, with the pET-20b(+)-asp                as the template, carrying out PCR amplification to                obtain the product, the aspartase gene with a T7                promoter and an rbs sequence at the 5' end; using                the genetic engineering method for cloning the gene                into the pET-20b(+)-asp to obtain the recombinant                plasmid pET-24a(+)-arg-asp or pET-20b(+)-asp;                transforming recombinant expression vector into                Escherichia coli BL21 (DE3) to obtain the                co-expressed genetic engineering strain BL21                (DE3)-pET24a(+)-arg-asp capable of synchronously                expressing highly active arginase and aspartase;                with the recombinant strain as the enzyme source,                in the same reaction system, catalyzing the                substrates Larginine and ammonium fumarate both at                concentration of 0.1-1 mol/l, where the amount of                the recombinant strain ranges from 4-6 g/l; at                30-45 degrees C and a pH of 8-10, with 1 mmol/l                anhydrous magnesium sulfate as the cofactor,                carrying out an enzymatic conversion reaction for                4-12 hours to produce L-ornithine and L-aspartic                acid; collecting the conversion solution, clearing                of large-molecular-weight impurities through a                hollow cellulose column with a molecular weight                cutoff of 10000, and decoloring with active carbon;                finally, decompressing, concentrating, mixing with                absolute ethyl alcohol 3 times its volume, and                separating out by stirring and cooling in an                ice-water bath; and collecting the precipitated                ornithine aspartate.
