CL  - WARSAW, POLAND
AB  - The purpose of our study was clinical and electrophysiological analysis of type and duration of seizures recorded by means of long-term video EEG monitoring, which enables to establish the diagnosis of psychogenic pseudoepileptic seizures occurring with or without epileptic seizures. Psychogenic pseudoepileptic seizures were diagnosed in 85/1083 patients (7.8%). Epileptic seizure duration was significantly shorter than psychogenic pseudoepileptic seizure duration. Our study has shown the difficulties involved in the diagnosis of psychogenic pseudoepileptic seizures and the negligible value of neuroimaging techniques and interictal EEG recordings in the differential diagnosis of epileptic versus nonepileptic seizures. Psychogenic seizures were significantly more frequent in women than in men. In our studies in the patient history we did not confirm sexual abuse as possible cause of psychogenic seizures.
