TI  - Composite structure for e.g. magnetic resonance            imaging in medical application, has shell partially            encapsulating core and made of non-organic material            such as silica, where shell has average thickness of            less than specific millimeters
AB  - 
                   NOVELTY - The structure has a core (16) comprising                magnetic nanoparticles (14) associated with quantum                dots (12) and partially encapsulated by a shell                (18). The shell has average thickness of less than                50 millimeters, and is made of non-organic material                such as silica or polyethylene glycol. A                passivating layer is formed on surface of each                quantum dot, where ratio of the quantum dots to the                magnetic nanoparticles is between 1 and 4. The                nanoparticles comprise ferric oxide, and the                quantum dots comprise cadmium selenide.
                    USE - Composite structure for bio-imaging and                biological labeling in biological application,                magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) and drug targeting                in medical application, or for an optical                communications system.
                    ADVANTAGE - The material of the shell can alter or improve                the properties such as water solubility, water                stability, photo-stability and biocompatibility.                The composition of the structure can be controlled                to provide desired magnetic and optical                properties.
                    DETAILED DESCRIPTION - An INDEPENDENT CLAIM is also included for a                method for making a composite structure.
