TI  - FEEDING VALUE OF FROST-DAMAGED SOYBEANS FOR LAMBS
T2  - JOURNAL OF ANIMAL SCIENCE
M3  - Article
AB  - Three experiments were conducted to determine the relative feeding value of frost-damaged soybeans [FDS] for ruminants. Frost-damaged soybean ether-extract content was variable and differed [P < .05] from mature soybeans. Isonitrogenous supplementation of corn silage diets with soybean meal [SBM], SBM + soybean oil [BO] mature raw soybeans [MSB], and FDS was compared in sheep. Acid detergent fiber and apparent N digestion were lower [P < .001]for supplements containing oil. Nitrogen retention was reduced [P < .07] only for raw soybean supplements. Ruminal NH3 N and branched-chain VFA concentrations differed [P < .01] between SBM and supplements containing oil. Maximum tolerable inclusion level of FDS in corn silage diets was tested in wethers using diets containing 0, 7, 14, or 21% FDS. Dry matter and ADF digestibility declined linearly [P < .01] with increasing dietary FDS. Ether extract digestibility was unchanged due to treatment, but GE digestibility decreased quadratically [P < .01]. The most pronounced decline in GE digestibility occurred when FDS increased from 14 to 21% of the diet. The effects of FDS on corn silage utilization were similar to MSB effects. Oil content and antinutritional factors contributed to detrimental effects. Frost-damaged soybeans should not exceed 14% of corn silage diets fed to growing wethers.
