TI  - The patterns of dysphagia according to the location and the volume of brain lesion in stroke patients
T2  - PROCEEDINGS OF THE 3RD WORLD CONGRESS OF THE INTERNATIONAL SOCIETY OF PHYSICAL AND REHABILITATION MEDICINE ISPRM
M3  - Proceedings Paper
CP  - 3rd World Congress of the International-Society-of-Physical-and-Rehabilitation-Medicine (ISPRM)
CL  - Sao Paulo, BRAZIL
AB  - The aim of this study was to investigate the patterns of dysphagia according to the location and the volume of brain lesion. Twenty six first-ever unilateral ischemic stroke patients with dysphagia were recruited. Videofluoroscopic Swallowing Study (VFSS) was performed to evaluate the dysphagia. The lesion locations and volume were assessed by brain MRI. As a result, there was no significant difference in oral phase dysfunction according to the lesion locations. And delayed swallowing reflex was least frequent and the frequency of aspiration was significantly low in subcortical lesion. The amount of residue in vallecular space and pyriform sinus was most plentiful in cortical lesion. But there was no significant correlation between cortical or subcotical. lesion and FDS according to the lesion volume. Although there was no significant correlation between the severity of dysphagia and the lesion volume, various oropharyngeal dysfunctions were found commonly in the patients with unilateral hemispheric stroke.
